From 82ac48c263a96bc36f106e1dee01ad389b77871f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 03:24:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] grep-searcher: document the rolling line buffer

Add a module-level overview to line_buffer.rs explaining the core idea
behind LineBuffer: how a fixed-size buffer searches an arbitrarily large
stream by exposing only complete lines, rolling the unconsumed remainder
to the front, tracking an absolute byte offset across rolls, and growing
when a single line exceeds capacity. The individual items were already
documented; this ties them together for a reader meeting the type for the
first time.

Comment-only: no code changes.
---
 crates/searcher/src/line_buffer.rs | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/searcher/src/line_buffer.rs b/crates/searcher/src/line_buffer.rs
index e73c650..abfce41 100644
--- a/crates/searcher/src/line_buffer.rs
+++ b/crates/searcher/src/line_buffer.rs
@@ -1,3 +1,30 @@
+/*!
+A fixed-size, rolling buffer for searching an arbitrarily large stream line by
+line without holding all of it in memory.
+
+The `ReadByLine` search strategy (in `searcher::glue`) uses this to search a
+[`std::io::Read`] source that may be far larger than the buffer. The core idea
+is that only *complete* lines are ever exposed for searching:
+
+* [`LineBuffer::buffer`] returns bytes only up to `last_lineterm`, the position
+  just past the final line terminator. Any trailing partial line (between
+  `last_lineterm` and `end`) is withheld until the rest of it is read.
+* After the caller has searched and consumed some prefix, [`LineBuffer::roll`]
+  slides the unconsumed remainder (the withheld partial line plus any retained
+  context) to the front of the buffer, and [`LineBuffer::fill`] reads more from
+  the source to append after it.
+
+An `absolute_byte_offset` is tracked across rolls so in-buffer offsets can be
+mapped back to positions in the original stream even though the bytes
+themselves move.
+
+When a single line is longer than the buffer's capacity, no complete line can
+be exposed; the buffer then *grows* according to its [`BufferAllocation`]
+policy (unbounded by default, or up to a configured limit, past which an error
+is returned). Binary detection (see [`BinaryDetection`]) is also applied here as
+data is read, so binary bytes are never observable by callers.
+*/
+
 use std::io;
 
 use bstr::ByteSlice;

